{% comment %}
  BreadcrumbList structured data.

  WHY THIS SNIPPET EXISTS. The theme already emits Product, Organization and
  WebSite schema, and emits no BreadcrumbList anywhere. That is the one Google
  renders inside the result itself: without it a product result shows the raw URL
  path, with it the result shows Store > Collection > Product. On a catalogue with
  thousands of deep URLs that is the difference between a result that reads as a
  category and one that reads as a slug.

  GOOGLE REQUIRES AT LEAST TWO ListItems, and this is where the first version of
  this file was wrong. It rendered from the layout on every template, so `index`,
  `cart`, `search`, `404`, `list-collections` and `customers/*` each emitted a
  BreadcrumbList containing exactly ONE item, the store name. Valid JSON,
  meaningless markup, and an invalid item reported sitewide in Search Console. The
  crumbs are now built into a list FIRST and the script is only emitted when a
  real trail exists.

  WHY THE MIDDLE CRUMB COMES FROM collection AND NOT THE URL. Shopify serves the
  same product under /products/x and /collections/y/products/x, and only the second
  knows which collection the customer came through. `collection` is set on that
  route and nil on the bare product URL, so the crumb appears exactly when it is
  true. Inventing a category from the handle would be structured data that
  contradicts the page, which is worse than none.

  THE LAST CRUMB USES canonical_url. On /collections/y/products/x the product URL
  and the page's own canonical disagree, and a breadcrumb whose final item points
  somewhere other than the canonical is the mismatch worth avoiding. Google also
  treats `item` as optional on the final crumb and falls back to the page URL, so
  emitting the canonical explicitly is the same answer stated out loud.

  ABSOLUTE URLS. `item` is an IRI, and a relative one resolves against the document
  base, which is ambiguous under a proxy, under AMP, and under syndication.
  `request.origin` gives the scheme and host without a hardcoded string to rot.

  ESCAPING. Every interpolated value goes through `| json`, which quotes AND
  escapes. A product title containing a double quote, which in a technical
  catalogue is routine because inches are written 2", would otherwise produce
  invalid JSON and take the whole block down with it.
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign crumb_names = ''
  assign crumb_urls = ''
  assign sep = '~|~'

  assign crumb_names = shop.name
  assign crumb_urls = request.origin | append: routes.root_url

  case template.name
    when 'product'
      if collection and collection.title != blank
        assign crumb_names = crumb_names | append: sep | append: collection.title
        assign crumb_urls = crumb_urls | append: sep | append: request.origin | append: collection.url
      endif
      assign crumb_names = crumb_names | append: sep | append: product.title
      assign crumb_urls = crumb_urls | append: sep | append: canonical_url
    when 'collection'
      if collection.title != blank
        assign crumb_names = crumb_names | append: sep | append: collection.title
        assign crumb_urls = crumb_urls | append: sep | append: canonical_url
      endif
    when 'article'
      if article
        assign crumb_names = crumb_names | append: sep | append: blog.title
        assign crumb_urls = crumb_urls | append: sep | append: request.origin | append: blog.url
        assign crumb_names = crumb_names | append: sep | append: article.title
        assign crumb_urls = crumb_urls | append: sep | append: canonical_url
      endif
    when 'blog'
      if blog.title != blank
        assign crumb_names = crumb_names | append: sep | append: blog.title
        assign crumb_urls = crumb_urls | append: sep | append: canonical_url
      endif
    when 'page'
      if page.title != blank
        assign crumb_names = crumb_names | append: sep | append: page.title
        assign crumb_urls = crumb_urls | append: sep | append: canonical_url
      endif
  endcase

  assign names = crumb_names | split: sep
  assign urls = crumb_urls | split: sep
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  One crumb is not a trail. Google requires two or more ListItems, so a page with
  nothing but the store name emits nothing at all rather than an invalid item.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if names.size > 1 -%}
<script type="application/ld+json">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  "itemListElement": [
    {%- for name in names -%}
      {%- unless forloop.first %},{% endunless -%}
      {
        "@type": "ListItem",
        "position": {{ forloop.index }},
        "name": {{ name | json }},
        "item": {{ urls[forloop.index0] | json }}
      }
    {%- endfor -%}
  ]
}
</script>
{%- endif -%}
